WEBVTT

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:03.990
The next few sections will
cover how to better cope

00:00:04.030 --> 00:00:08.070
with tinnitus using three main
groups of coping strategies.

00:00:08.230 --> 00:00:11.095
In this section, we will
define stress and discuss

00:00:11.135 --> 00:00:14.770
how to analyze the stress
that you are experiencing.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:17.303
This section will be
followed by three sections

00:00:17.343 --> 00:00:20.850
that will teach different
types of coping strategies.

00:00:20.890 --> 00:00:23.970
So first, let's talk about stress.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:28.934
Just as everyone experiences
stress in a variety of ways,

00:00:28.974 --> 00:00:32.030
we all cope with stress in different ways.

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.932
The coping framework used
in this course is based

00:00:34.972 --> 00:00:39.450
on a program called Coping
Effectiveness Training, or CET.

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:44.179
CET was developed by Chesney
and colleagues to coach individuals

00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:48.070
in the best ways to optimally
manage various health conditions.

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:52.563
We are teaching a version that has been
tested by multiple scientific studies,

00:00:52.603 --> 00:00:58.250
see Martz and Chesney studies in the reference
list, to help people better cope.

00:00:58.570 --> 00:01:03.030
Coping with tinnitus in particular has
been the focus of two scientific studies.

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:05.267
We will now cover some
definitions of stress,

00:01:05.307 --> 00:01:08.830
and then we will teach you
about coping with stress.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:13.170
What is stress?

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:17.530
We all know what stress feels like
from our own personal experience.

00:01:17.570 --> 00:01:20.970
Stress is a natural part of living.

00:01:21.670 --> 00:01:24.123
Generally, stress can
be thought of as tension

00:01:24.163 --> 00:01:28.110
or pressure that causes
a person to make changes.

00:01:28.230 --> 00:01:30.268
It can result in changes we want,

00:01:30.308 --> 00:01:35.710
as well as changes that we do not
want, like the onset of tinnitus.

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:40.794
For a situation to be stressful,
it must matter to the person

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:44.430
and be a situation that he
or she cannot handle with ease.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:47.741
Normal human responses
or reactions to stress

00:01:47.781 --> 00:01:51.830
are often grouped into
three broad categories.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:56.010
Fight, such as anger
or attacking responses.

00:01:56.690 --> 00:01:59.750
Flight, such as fear or avoidance.

00:02:00.070 --> 00:02:03.990
Or freeze,
such as helplessness or depression.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:09.507
The symptoms of stress can take many forms
and be expressed in a range of physical,

00:02:09.547 --> 00:02:12.350
emotional, cognitive,
or behavioral symptoms.

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:17.730
These symptoms may be the first
clues that you are under stress.

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:22.949
For example, physical symptoms,
shortness of breath, pounding heart,

00:02:22.989 --> 00:02:28.929
stiff or tense muscles, headaches,
upset stomach, clenching your jaw or fists,

00:02:28.969 --> 00:02:33.221
dizziness, trembling, diarrhea,
grinding your teeth, sweating,

00:02:33.261 --> 00:02:38.290
feeling faint, losing interest in sex,
tiredness, or restlessness.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.252
Emotional symptoms,
irritability, anger, worry,

00:02:42.292 --> 00:02:46.481
trouble concentrating,
negative outlook on life, loneliness,

00:02:46.521 --> 00:02:52.070
feeling down or depressed, feeling
tense or grouchy or unable to relax.

00:02:52.330 --> 00:02:57.268
Behavioral symptoms, overeating,
losing appetite, trouble sleeping,

00:02:57.308 --> 00:03:01.797
being prone to accidents,
using more alcohol, avoiding tasks,

00:03:01.837 --> 00:03:05.811
sleep problems, trouble
completing work assignments,

00:03:05.851 --> 00:03:10.590
fidgeting, tremors, crying,
or an increase in smoking.

00:03:11.410 --> 00:03:15.794
Cognitive symptoms, being anxious,
having racing or slowed thoughts,

00:03:15.834 --> 00:03:21.150
fearful anticipation, poor concentration,
or difficulty with your memory.

00:03:21.190 --> 00:03:27.296
Social behaviors, avoiding others,
isolating, not wanting to be alone,

00:03:27.336 --> 00:03:32.310
being angry at others,
or getting easily irritated by others.

00:03:32.710 --> 00:03:37.370
Do you recognize any of your typical
stress-related symptoms from this list?

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.257
Think about whether you
experience other symptoms

00:03:40.297 --> 00:03:43.690
of stress that we have
not included on this list.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:49.720
People differ in what matters to them.

00:03:49.880 --> 00:03:53.960
For example, being criticized
on the job may matter a great

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:58.180
deal to one person but mean
little to another person.

00:03:58.720 --> 00:04:02.029
Different things are stressful
for different people largely because

00:04:02.069 --> 00:04:05.568
the meaning of the event differs
between people and because people

00:04:05.608 --> 00:04:09.720
have different resources available
for coping or dealing with stress.

00:04:10.180 --> 00:04:14.120
People also differ in the way that they
respond to stressful situations.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.460
What is difficult for one person to manage
may be easy for another person to manage.

00:04:20.620 --> 00:04:25.640
It is not wrong or bad to feel
stressed when someone else does not.

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:29.960
People are simply stressed
by different kinds of things.

00:04:30.460 --> 00:04:32.320
What makes you stressed?

00:04:32.820 --> 00:04:35.753
Pause for a moment
and think about your life

00:04:35.793 --> 00:04:39.840
as a whole and what causes
you the most stress.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.643
Now think about recent
stressful situations

00:04:47.683 --> 00:04:52.540
and what was the most
stressful situation for you.

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:58.546
Keep that in mind as we go
through the next few sections

00:04:58.586 --> 00:05:02.930
and discuss coping strategies
for different types of stress.

00:05:04.980 --> 00:05:11.560
When most people feel stressed,
they often use very general terms such as,

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:28.700
Many of us speak about stress using terms
that focus on the general condition.

00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:35.200
So the first step for better managing
stress is to get specific about the stress.

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:41.640
Break down any general stressful
condition into specific situations.

00:05:41.840 --> 00:05:46.529
Specific situations typically provide
more information about the stress

00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:52.740
and we have more control over specific
kinds of stress than general stress.

00:05:55.060 --> 00:06:00.080
Ask yourself these kinds of questions
when the stressful situation occurs.

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.900
What is stressful about this situation?

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:06.660
When does it occur?

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:08.660
Where does it happen?

00:06:08.740 --> 00:06:12.020
What causes the stressful situation?

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.620
Why should you get specific about
the stress you are experiencing?

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:19.965
As mentioned, we may
have more control over

00:06:20.005 --> 00:06:23.460
specific kinds of stress
than general stress.

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:28.624
In addition, an important strategy that we
can teach in this course is to match

00:06:28.664 --> 00:06:34.700
the type of stress that you are experiencing
with a specific category of coping.

00:06:35.060 --> 00:06:38.794
We will give you more details
about the different types of coping

00:06:38.834 --> 00:06:43.380
categories or strategies in the next
few sections of this course.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:48.320
First, let us go through some examples.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:50.258
For these 11 situations,

00:06:50.298 --> 00:06:57.020
note that 5 involve general stress
and 6 involve specific stress.

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:01.080
Here are some general
stressful conditions.
